  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe thunderstorms and some instances of \nflash flooding are possible on Sunday.\n\nOvernight model guidance has come into a broad consensus\nregarding the synoptic evolution for Sunday.\n\nIn the upper levels, a belt of seasonably strong westerlies \nwill persist from the upper Great Lakes across the Saint \nLawrence Valley into the Northeast and Canadian Maritimes. Broad\nascent on the equatorward side of this jet will persist much of\nthe time from early Sunday morning through late Sunday night.\n\nMeanwhile, a pair of mid-level shortwaves, likely augmented by \nupstream convection over the Midwest and Ohio Valley, will track\nacross the Mid-Atlantic on Sunday. Although timing remains a \nbit uncertain, most guidance now agrees on an initial wave and \nattendant enhanced lift/flow during the morning. This initial \nwave may slow on its exit during the afternoon, only to be \nfollowed by another stout wave during the late afternoon and \nevening. W/NW flow in the 700-500 hPa layer increases to 30-40\nkts, possibly higher depending on how these waves are deepened\nby convection that occurs today/tonight upstream.\n\nIn the low levels, flow will also increase. This will allow a sharp \nrise in theta-e, with PWs rising from around 1.75\" at 03Z Sun to \nnear 2.25\" by 09Z Sun. Further increases are progged through Sun \nevening. Low-level/steering flow will lie parallel to a slowly \nnorthward lifting warm front expected to bisect the area, so despite \nthe increased flow, backbuilding/repeating convection seems \nplausible. Training plus the high PW airmass yields a threat for \nexcessive rain and flooding, perhaps mitigated a bit by ongoing \ndrought east of the Appalachians.\n\nAreas that have seen recent heavy rain, or typical poor\ndrainage/urban areas would be rather susceptible to any such\nexcessive rainfall amounts. Of note, the 00Z HREF 3-hr PMM has\n50-70% chance of exceeding 1\" in the vicinity of western MD\nearly Sunday morning as a presumably weakening MCS treks into\nthe region. The probs for 3\" are less than 10%, but recent heavy\nrain in this area could raise at least a conditional flood\nconcern as early as the predawn hours on Sunday. Probs for 1\"\nand 3\" in 3 hours are notably higher east of the Blue Ridge\nduring the afternoon hours. The latest HREF only goes out until\nearly Sunday evening, but guidance such as the NAM and extended\nRAP suggest a further increase in PWs, low-level flow, and\nSBCAPE Sunday night suggesting a lingering heavy rain threat.\n\nIn addition to any heavy rain threat is the potential for bands\nof strong to severe thunderstorms. Increased flow down into the\nlow levels would be favorable for organized line segments\ncapable of mixing down damaging wind gusts in any organizing\ncold pools. The saturated profiles could result in water loading\ndowndrafts, though the lack of mid-level dry air may keep DCAPE\nlow putting a ceiling on just how high damaging wind gusts can\nget (most likely in the 50-65 mph range). The increased low-\nlevel flow and SRH coupled with moist profiles could lend to the\nthreat of a few tornadoes as well.\n\nAlthough the prospect of early morning clouds and precip may at\nfirst glance seem to preclude a higher potential for heating and\nsevere weather later in the day, the high low-level moisture\ncontent coupled with steady height falls aloft means that\nheating will not need to be as robust to result in sufficient\nCAPE for strong to severe convection, especially given the shear\nthat will be present.\n\nSome severe weather or flood threat may persist into the night\ngiven the above listed factors.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonably warm conditions and daily shower and\nthunderstorm chances are expected through Monday, before \nanother round of storms are possible on Thursday.\n\nHigh pressure building across southern Quebec down into New\nEngland will favor a mostly quiet day for the area today. An \nonshore component to the winds should favor a mix of clouds and \nsun through the day. Highs will rise into the mid/upper 80s with\nany shower/thunderstorm chances confined to the Potomac \nHighlands across the central Shenandoah Valley toward I-64. \nRecent high-resolution models are showing an MCS racing across \nthe Ohio Valley late today. Some of this could leak across the \nAlleghenies, but confidence remains low.\n\nAs Sunday's cold front tracks offshore, upper-level zonal flow \nbringing drier air from the Great Lakes persists over the Mid-\nAtlantic through midweek, creating a break in the rain and \nthunderstorms. While temperatures and dewpoints drop a few \ndegrees by Tuesday in the wake of the front, temperatures should\ngradually increase again in the latter half of the week as high\npressure builds overhead. Prior to high pressure building,\nadditional convection is possible Monday but with a likely\nlesser threat of severe weather or flooding.\n\nMeanwhile, an upper-level trough will begin to form over the \nNorthern Plains and then track across the Great Lakes late in the \nweek, with a strong cold fro",
